  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: Midlevel flow remains somewhat zonal across the \nGreat Lakes Region with upstream weak troughing over the \nNorthwestern United States. Surface low pressure currently over the \ncentral CONUS will progress northeast across the Ohio Valley this \nafternoon/evening, supporting light rainfall to the Northwoods. \n\nThe midlevel pattern develops a quasi-split flow set up this Monday \nwith weak southerly flow across the mid/southern Mississippi Valley \nand Northern Flow extending up to Northern Canada. The remainder of \nthe week will remain benign across the Midwest as broad midlevel \nridging to the north develops through Friday, supporting surface \nhigh pressure and seasonably normal temperatures. Some low-end \nchances of precipitation will likely enter the forecast next weekend \nas a weak cyclone drags a frontal boundary across the 45th parallel, \nbut confidence on any appreciable precipitation remains low at this \ntime. \n\nForecast Details: \n\nToday and Sunday...Previously mentioned low pressure currently over \nthe Central U.S this morning will continue to develop and deepen \nthis afternoon. A narrow band of frontogenesis will centralize the \nstrongest forcing to our southwest where Wisconsin and Illinois will \nlikely observe several inches, but much less is expected over our \nCWA. Latest high-resolution guidance keeps measurable rainfall south \nof the M-32 corridor, with highest amounts of a general quarter to \nhalf inch along and south of Grand Traverse Bay. Showers will taper \noff overnight as low pressure progresses downstream. A few showers \nwith patchy fog remain possible Sunday morning but will burn off \nquickly as upstream high pressure begins to build. \n\n\nMonday through Saturday Outlook...Broad midlevel ridging to our \nnorth builds this Monday to return quiet weather Monday through \nFriday. Surface high pressure with H8 temperatures in the single \ndigits will support mostly sunny skies with highs in the 60s most \ndays and chilly overnight lows in the 40s for most areas. Only \nchance of precipitation remains at the end of the forecast period as \na weak low pressure system develops over northern Canada and \nprogresses eastward. An aforementioned weak frontal boundary will \nattempt to return low end chances of rainfall, but the long range \npattern is expected to remain dry overall as this split flow pattern \nkeeps any disturbances north or south of the Great Lakes Region.",
